{"task_id": "Mbpp/67", "solution": "def bell_number(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the nth Bell number, which represents the number of ways to partition a set of n elements.\n    This implementation uses dynamic programming based on the Bell triangle.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # Initialize two rows for the Bell triangle: previous_row and current_row\n    # We only need to store the values for the current and previous iterations,\n    # making space complexity O(n).\n    previous_row = [0] * (n + 1)\n    current_row = [0] * (n + 1)\n\n    # Base case for the Bell triangle: T(0,0) = 1\n    previous_row[0] = 1\n\n    # Fill the triangle row by row up to n\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        # The first element of the current row (T(i,0)) is the last element of the previous row (T(i-1, i-1))\n        current_row[0] = previous_row[i - 1]\n        \n        # Fill the rest of the current row using the recurrence T(i,j) = T(i-1, j-1) + T(i, j-1)\n        for j in range(1, i + 1):\n            current_row[j] = previous_row[j - 1] + current_row[j - 1]\n        \n        # The current_row becomes the previous_row for the next iteration\n        previous_row = list(current_row)\n\n    # The nth Bell number is the first element of the nth row (current_row[0] after n iterations)\n    return current_row[0]"}
