{"task_id": "Mbpp/74", "solution": "def is_samepatterns(words, patterns):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if two arrays of strings follow the same pattern of element occurrences.\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1. Length Check\n    if len(words) != len(patterns):\n        return False\n\n    # 2. Build First-Occurrence Maps\n    # These maps store the index of the first occurrence of each unique element\n    # in their respective sequences.\n    first_occurrence_words = {}\n    first_occurrence_patterns = {}\n\n    for i in range(len(words)):\n        word = words[i]\n        char = patterns[i]\n\n        if word not in first_occurrence_words:\n            first_occurrence_words[word] = i\n        if char not in first_occurrence_patterns:\n            first_occurrence_patterns[char] = i\n\n    # 3. Bijective Mapping Verification and 4. First-Occurrence Index Matching\n    # We use two dictionaries to track the one-to-one (bijective) mapping\n    # between elements of `words` and `patterns`.\n    word_to_pattern_map = {}  # Maps elements from words to patterns\n    pattern_to_word_map = {}  # Maps elements from patterns to words\n\n    for i in range(len(words)):\n        word = words[i]\n        char = patterns[i]\n\n        # Verify bijective mapping:\n        # Check consistency for word -> pattern direction\n        if word in word_to_pattern_map:\n            if word_to_pattern_map[word] != char:\n                return False\n        # Check consistency for pattern -> word direction\n        elif char in pattern_to_word_map: # This means `word` is new, but `char` is already mapped to another `word`.\n            return False                   # Violates bijective property.\n        else: # Both `word` and `char` are new to the current mappings, establish them.\n            word_to_pattern_map[word] = char\n            pattern_to_word_map[char] = word\n\n        # No need for a separate `if char in pattern_to_word_map` block\n        # as the first `if/elif/else` structure ensures mutual mapping and catches violations.\n        # However, to explicitly cover the symmetric check as described for bijective:\n        if char in pattern_to_word_map:\n            if pattern_to_word_map[char] != word:\n                return False\n\n        # 4. First-Occurrence Index Matching:\n        # This verifies that the elements at the current position (word, char)\n        # have their first occurrences at the same relative index in their respective sequences.\n        if first_occurrence_words[word] != first_occurrence_patterns[char]:\n            return False\n\n    # 5. Return Result: If all checks pass for all elements, the patterns are the same.\n    return True"}
